==English==
{{wikipedia|Trooper (rank)}}

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|troop|er}} < {{etyl|fr}} {{term|troupe|lang=fr}}.

Sense of cavalry soldier attested 1640, mounted policeman 1858 (Australia), state policeman 1911 (US).  The sense of "one who endures adversity" comes from English {{term|trouper||member of an acting troupe}}, 1959, but through assimilation with the sense of "soldier" has come to be usually spelled "trooper".

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|uːpə(r)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{military}} A soldier of private rank in [[cavalry]] or [[armour]].  Abbreviated [[Tpr]].
# A [[cavalry]] horse; [[charger]].
# A [[soldier]].
# {{British}} A [[troopship]].
# {{US}} A [[state trooper]].
# One who endures adversity or hardship with an attitude of [[stoicism]] and [[persistence]].
#: ''He was a real '''trooper''' about taking care of the kids for the weekend.''

